# Configuration for {{ engine.container }}, written by Unihelm.
#
# Redis and Valkey both read this file — Valkey is a fork of Redis and its
# configuration grammar is the same one, so there is one template rather than
# two that would drift apart.
#
# WHY THE PASSWORD IS IN A FILE AND NOT ANYWHERE ELSE. `--requirepass <secret>`
# on the server's command line, and a `REDIS_PASSWORD` in its environment, are
# both readable out of `/proc` by any account on this machine — and every
# tenant's PHP process and every tenant shell is an account on this machine.
# That is the exact boundary the password exists to draw, so the credential
# travels the one way that does not cross it: a file the host keeps in a
# `0700 root:root` directory (see `paths::engine_config_dir`) and mounts into
# the container read-only.
#
# Edits to this file are overwritten the next time the engine is installed, and
# a running container will not see them at all: the mount follows the inode
# this file had when the container started, and the panel replaces the file
# rather than rewriting it in place.

# The whole point. Without it every account on this server can run `KEYS *`
# against every other customer's sessions, and `FLUSHALL` the lot.
requirepass {{ engine.password }}

# Persistence. This was an argument to the server before the password needed a
# file, and it has to stay somewhere: the container is given a data volume, and
# an append-only file is what makes that volume hold anything. Without it the
# volume stays empty and "your data survives a restart" would be a lie.
appendonly yes

# Where that append-only file goes: the volume's mount point, said explicitly
# rather than inherited from the image's working directory, so a base image
# that changes its `WORKDIR` cannot quietly move a customer's data onto the
# container's own writable layer — where a restart discards it.
dir {{ engine.data_dir }}
